['Here's an example implementation in ASP.NET Core using Entity Framework as an ORM and Serilog for logging. This solution assumes a resource has an Id property of type int. 

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class ResourceController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly MyDbContext _context;
    private readonly ILogger<ResourceController> _logger;

    public ResourceController(MyDbContext context, ILogger<ResourceController> logger)
    {
        _context = context;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    [HttpPut("bulk-update")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> BulkUpdate([FromBody] IDictionary<int, Resource> resourcesToUpdate)
    {
        var ids = new HashSet<int>(resourcesToUpdate.Keys);
        var existingResources = await _context.Resources.Where(r => ids.Contains(r.Id)).ToListAsync();

        foreach (var resource in existingResources)
        {
            if (!resourcesToUpdate.TryGetValue(resource.Id, out var newResource)) continue;

            // Update properties of the existing resource with those from the new resource
            resource.Property1 = newResource.Property1;
            resource.Property2 = newResource.Property2;
        }

        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
        _logger.LogInformation("Bulk update operation completed");

        return Ok();
    }

    [HttpDelete]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Delete([FromBody] int[] ids)
    {
        var deletedCount = 0;
        foreach (var id in ids)
        {
            try
            {
                var resource = await _context.Resources.FindAsync(id);
                if (resource != null) 
                    _context.Resources.Remove(resource);

                ++deletedCount;
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                _logger.LogError(ex, $"Failed to delete resource with id:{id}");
            }
        }

        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
        _logger.LogInformation("Bulk deletion operation completed");

        return Ok($"Successfully deleted {deletedCount} resources.");
    }
}
```

This is a basic example and doesn't include validation, error handling or transactional behavior. In a real-world application you would likely have more comprehensive error checking (including handling resource not found errors), input validation, use of transactions for bulk operations to ensure atomicity and performance improvements like using "attach" or "entry state" in EF Core for updating resources.
']

['Here's an implementation using C# and .NET Core's XmlDocument class, which supports namespace-aware queries:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Xml;
using System.Linq;

public static Dictionary<string, List<XmlNode>> ParseXMLByName(string xmlString, string elementName, int limit)
{
    var doc = new XmlDocument();
    try
    {
        doc.LoadXml(xmlString);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        throw new Exception("Failed to load XML document", ex);
    }

    var namespaceManager = new XmlNamespaceManager(doc.NameTable);
    var nodes = doc.DocumentElement.SelectNodes($"//*[local-name()='{elementName}']");
    if (nodes == null || nodes.Count == 0)
        throw new Exception("No elements found in XML document.");

    var dict = new Dictionary<string, List<XmlNode>>();
    for(int i = 0; i < Math.Min(limit, nodes.Count); i++)
    {
        var node = nodes[i];

        // Combine the attributes to a string key
        var attributeKey = string.Join("_", node.Attributes.Cast<XmlAttribute>().OrderBy(a => a.Name).Select(a => $"{a.Name}={a.Value}"));

        if (dict.ContainsKey(attributeKey))
            dict[attributeKey].Add(node);
        else 
            dict[attributeKey] = new List<XmlNode> { node };
    }

    return dict;
}
```
This function takes an XML string, a name of the element to search for and a limit on the number of elements processed in a single query. It loads the document into an XmlDocument object, then finds all nodes with that name using XPath (with namespace awareness). It checks if any were found and throws an exception if not. 

If elements are found, it creates a dictionary where each key is a unique combination of attributes in sorted order (to handle attribute ordering), and the value is a list of matching nodes sharing those attributes. The function processes at most limit elements to prevent memory exhaustion. Finally, it returns the resulting dictionary.
']

['Here is how you can achieve this:

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class MD5HashGenerator {
    private final ScheduledExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor();
    private volatile ConcurrentMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void processBatch(List<String> batch) {
        for (String str : batch) {
            if (!cache.containsKey(str)) {
                computeAndCacheMD5Async(str);
            }
        }
    }

    private void computeAndCacheMD5Async(String input) {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            try {
                MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
                byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
                StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
                for (byte b : hashBytes) {
                    sb.append(String.format("%02x", b & 0xff));
                }
                cache.putIfAbsent(input, sb.toString());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Handle exception gracefully here
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        });
    }

    public void refreshCache() {
        executor.scheduleWithFixedDelay(() -> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(), 0, 1, TimeUnit.MINUTES);
    }
}
```
This code creates a `MD5HashGenerator` class which processes batches of strings and computes their MD5 hashes. The results are stored in a concurrent hash map for thread safety and efficient lookup. To optimize large input sizes, the computation is done asynchronously using an executor service. Additionally, to avoid redundant computations, we check if the string is already in the cache before computing its MD5 hash.

To refresh the cache at regular intervals, we schedule a task with `scheduleWithFixedDelay` that will create a new concurrent map and replace the old one every minute.

Please note that this code does not handle exceptions in many places for simplicity. In a real world application, you'd want to catch specific exceptions and provide more meaningful error messages. Also, please make sure that MD5 is suitable for your use case before using it. Consider stronger hashing algorithms like SHA-256 if security is a concern.
']

