['Below is a simple implementation using Java 8 Stream API to process large data files in a memory-efficient way. This code does not include any exception handling or logging time as these require separate libraries and are dependent on the specific use case. Also, it does not include any file reading or outlier detection logic which would be more complex than this simple example. Please provide more specific requirements for exception handling, logging time and outlier detection logic.

```java
import java.time.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.io.IOException;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.*;

class UserInteraction {
    String userId;
    LocalDateTime timestamp;

    // Constructor, getters and setters are omitted for brevity.
}

public class LogAnalyzer {
    public static final Duration OUTLIER_THRESHOLD = Duration.ofHours(1);

    private Map<String, List<Duration>> analyzeLogFile(Path logFile) throws IOException {
        try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(logFile)) {
            return lines
                .map(line -> line.split("\t")) // Assuming tab-separated values.
                .map(parts -> new UserInteraction(parts[0], LocalDateTime.parse(parts[1])))
                .collect(groupingBy(UserInteraction::getUserId))
                .entrySet()
                .stream()
                .collect(toMap(
                    Map.Entry::getKey, 
                    entry -> analyzeSessions(sortInteractions(entry.getValue()))));
        }
    }

    private List<Duration> sortInteractions(List<UserInteraction> interactions) {
        List<LocalDateTime> sortedTimestamps = interactions.stream()
            .map(UserInteraction::getTimestamp)
            .sorted()
            .collect(toList());

        return IntStream.range(0, sortedTimestamps.size()-1)
            .mapToObj(i -> Duration.between(sortedTimestamps.get(i), sortedTimestamps.get(i+1)))
            .collect(toList());
    }

    private Map<String, Object> analyzeSessions(List<Duration> sessionDurations) {
        double average = sessionDurations.stream()
            .mapToLong(Duration::toMillis)
            .average().orElse(0);

        List<Duration> outliers = sessionDurations.stream()
            .filter(duration -> duration.compareTo(OUTLIER_THRESHOLD) > 0)
            .collect(toList());

        Map<String, Object> result = new HashMap<>();
        result.put("averageDuration", Duration.ofMillis((long) average));
        result.put("outliers", outliers);
        return result;
    }
}
```
Please note that this is just a simple demonstration and can be improved in many ways, including adding error handling (e.g., with `try-catch` blocks), better data validation, and so forth. Also, it does not provide the exact memory usage characteristics due to JVM heap size limitations and other factors which make Java unable to process all records at once from a file. 

For such cases you would need more sophisticated solutions that involve using databases (like Hadoop or Spark), distributed systems, or cloud-based data processing services.
']

['Here is an example of how you might structure your Java NIO channel connection pool with some basic connection failure handling:

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.*;

public class ConnectionPool {
    private Map<SelectionKey, SocketChannel> channels = new HashMap<>();
    private Selector selector;

    public void init() throws IOException {
        selector = Selector.open();
    }

    public SelectionKey register(SocketChannel channel) throws ClosedChannelException {
        channel.configureBlocking(false);
        return channel.register(selector, 0);
    }

    public void processConnections() throws IOException {
        int num = selector.selectNow(); // use select instead to wait for connections

        if (num == 0) {
            removeIdleChannels();
            return;
        }

        Set<SelectionKey> keys = selector.selectedKeys();
        Iterator<SelectionKey> iterator = keys.iterator();

        while(iterator.hasNext()) {
            SelectionKey key = iterator.next();

            if (key.isConnectable()) {
                handleConnectionEvent(key);
            } else if (key.isReadable() || key.isWritable()) {
                SocketChannel channel = (SocketChannel) key.channel();

                try {
                    // perform read/write operations on the channel
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    handleConnectionFailure(key, channel);
                }
            }

            iterator.remove();
        }
    }

    private void handleConnectionEvent(SelectionKey key) throws IOException {
        SocketChannel channel = (SocketChannel) key.channel();

        if (channel.finishConnect()) {
            channels.put(key, channel); // save the connected channel for future use
        } else {
            handleConnectionFailure(key, channel);
        }
    }

    private void handleConnectionFailure(SelectionKey key, SocketChannel channel) throws IOException {
        System.out.println("Failed to connect to " + channel.getRemoteAddress());

        // clean up the failed connection and retry
        channels.remove(key);
        key.cancel();
        channel.close();
    }

    private void removeIdleChannels() {
        Iterator<SelectionKey> iterator = channels.keySet().iterator();

        while (iterator.hasNext()) {
            SelectionKey key = iterator.next();

            if (!key.isValid()) {
                iterator.remove(); // remove idle connections from the pool
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This is a basic implementation and doesn't include many features like connection caching, connection timeout handling or load balancing. For production-ready code you would likely want to use an established library for these features. Also note that this does not address threading concerns - if you need to handle connections from multiple threads, you might need to add synchronization blocks around the channel map.
']

