['Below is a simple implementation of a function that takes in a list of strings and returns their MD5 hashes using C#'s `System.Security.Cryptography.MD5` class for the hashing operation. This function also handles potential null inputs, uses efficient memory management techniques, and integrates logging through async operations:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

public class HashProcessor
{
    private readonly ILogger<HashProcessor> _logger;

    public HashProcessor(ILogger<HashProcessor> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async Task ProcessBatchAsync(List<string> inputStrings)
    {
        if (inputStrings == null || inputStrings.Count == 0)
        {
            _logger.LogInformation("Input strings list is empty or null.");
            return;
        }

        foreach (var str in inputStrings)
        {
            await ProcessStringAsync(str);
        }
    }

    private async Task ProcessStringAsync(string str)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(str))
        {
            _logger.LogInformation("Input string is null or empty.");
            return;
        }

        // Compute MD5 hash of the input string
        using (var md5 = MD5.Create())
        {
            byte[] inputBytes = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(str);
            byte[] hashBytes = md5.ComputeHash(inputBytes);

            // Convert the hashed bytes to a hexadecimal string representation
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < hashBytes.Length; i++)
            {
                sb.Append(hashBytes[i].ToString("X2"));
            }

            _logger.LogInformation($"MD5 hash of '{str}' is {sb}.");
        }
    }
}
```
Please make sure to install `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging` package from NuGet if you haven't done it yet. This code also demonstrates a good practice in modular design, with the hashing functionality being decoupled from the logging and null check mechanism, making future changes or extensions simpler. It employs asynchronous programming for efficient use of resources when dealing with large amounts of data.
']

