['Here is a Java code snippet implementing your requirements using Java 8's Stream API for processing file content and a try-with-resources statement to ensure the `ObjectInputStream` is properly closed:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class FileModifierAndLoader {
    private static final String DEFAULT_EXTENSION = ".ext";

    public Object modifyAndLoadFilename(String filename) {
        if (filename.length() > 255) {
            filename = filename.substring(0, 255);
        }

        int dotIndex = filename.lastIndexOf('.');
        String extension;
        if (dotIndex == -1) {
            extension = DEFAULT_EXTENSION;
        } else {
            extension = filename.substring(dotIndex);
        }

        long sequence = 0L;
        try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(Paths.get(filename))) {
            String content = lines.collect(Collectors.joining());
            for (char c : content.toCharArray()) {
                if (!Character.isWhitespace(c)) {
                    sequence += c;
                }
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
            return null;
        }

        String newFilename = filename + extension + String.format("%05d", sequence % 100000);
        Object loadedObject = null;
        try (FileInputStream fileIn = new FileInputStream(newFilename);
             ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(fileIn)) {
            loadedObject = in.readObject();
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
            return null;
        }

        if (loadedObject instanceof MoSTState) {
            return loadedObject;
        } else {
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```

This code checks the length of the filename and truncates it to 255 characters if necessary. It then determines whether there is an extension present in the filename, extracts it if possible, or uses a default extension otherwise. The method calculates a unique sequence by summing up all non-whitespace characters' ASCII values from the file content and formats it into a string of five digits. A new filename with this calculated sequence is constructed. 

The code then tries to open an `ObjectInputStream` for the newly created filename, read in the loaded object, and checks whether it is an instance of `MoSTState`. If all these operations are successful and if the loaded object is indeed a `MoSTState` object, the method returns this object; otherwise, null is returned.
']

