["To solve this problem with efficiency and manageable resources, we can use NodeJS's built-in 'fs' and 'events' modules along with some third party libraries like 'LRU Cache', which are perfect for caching large files in memory. We'll also use 'throat' to limit concurrent read operations:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const events = require('events');
const LRUCache = require('lru-cache');
const throat = require('throat');

class FileReader extends events.EventEmitter {
  constructor(maxSize) {
    super();
    this.cache = new LRUCache({ max: maxSize });
    // The function for reading files asynchronously throttled by 10 at a time to prevent memory leaks
    this._readFileAsyncThrottled = throat(10)(this._readFileAsync);
  }

  async readFile(path) {
    let content;

    // If the file is in the cache, retrieve it directly from there
    if (this.cache.has(path)) {
      this.emit('hitCache');
      content = this.cache.get(path);
    } else {  // Else read the file normally
      try {
        const stat = await fs.promises.stat(path);

        if (stat.size > maxSize) throw new Error('File size exceeded');

        content = await this._readFileAsyncThrottled(path);
        // Store the file in cache
        this.cache.set(path, content);
      } catch (error) {
        if (error.code === 'ENOENT') {  // If the file does not exist
          throw new Error('File does not exist');
        } else if (error.message === 'File size exceeded') {  // If the file size is too large
          throw error;
        } else {  // Other errors
          throw new Error(`Failed to read the file: ${error}`);
        }
      }
    }

    this.emit('endRead', path);
    return content;
  }

  async _readFileAsync(path) {
    let data = '';

    const rl = fs.createReadStream(path, { highWaterMark: 1024 }); // Adjust the highWaterMark according to your needs

    rl.on('data', chunk => {
      data += chunk;
      this.emit('progress', (chunk.length / rl.readableLength * 100).toFixed(2));
    }).on('end', () => {
      console.log('End of stream');
    });

    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      rl.on('error', reject);
      rl.on('end', () => resolve(data));
    });
  }
}
```
This class reads a file asynchronously and caches its content in memory using LRU-cache, which automatically removes least recently used items when the cache is full. This should give us good performance benefits.

The 'progress' event allows you to track how much of the file has been read so far, giving an indication of how long the operation will take. 

This solution does not implement automatic rotation and purging of cached files as it heavily depends on your specific use case and needs. If this is needed, a simple solution might be to keep track of when each item was last accessed or updated in the cache and periodically purge items that haven't been accessed recently. 

Lastly, please note that NodeJS itself uses an event-driven, non-blocking I/O model which makes it lightweight and efficient for data-intensive real-time applications running across distributed devices. It implements this using C++, not JavaScript, but the concept remains largely the same.
"]

