["The given task is very complex and requires a deep understanding of concurrency handling in JavaScript, data stream management, and fault tolerance and monitoring systems. Here's an attempt at solving this problem with simple pseudo-code. Due to the complexity of the question, a complete solution isn't feasible here, but I can provide you with some general structure which could be used:

```javascript
class GraphDatabaseManager {
  constructor(configService) {
    this.vertices = [];
    this.edges = [];
    this.configService = configService;
    this.queue = new StreamQueue();
    this.logger = new Logger();
    this.isPaused = false;
  }

  addData(data) {
    if (this.isPaused) return;

    this.queue.enqueue(data);
    this.processQueue();
  }

  pause() {
    this.isPaused = true;
  }

  resume() {
    this.isPaused = false;
    this.processQueue();
  }

  async processQueue() {
    while (!this.queue.isEmpty && !this.isPaused) {
      const data = this.queue.dequeue();

      try {
        const config = await this.configService.getConfig(data);

        if (data.type === 'vertex') {
          this.vertices.push({...config, ...data});
        } else if (data.type === 'edge') {
          this.edges.push({...config, ...data});
        }
      } catch(err) {
        this.logger.error('Error processing data:', err);

        // Add the same data back to queue for later retry
        this.queue.enqueue(data);

        // Pause data ingestion for a moment
        this.pause();
        setTimeout(() => {
          this.resume();
        }, 1000);
      }
    }
  }
}
```
This is a simplified example, you'd likely need to add more sophisticated handling of concurrency and conflict resolution in the `processQueue` method based on the specifics of your system, but this should give you an idea of how to approach it. It also assumes that there is some kind of asynchronous configuration service with error detection that rejects promises on failure. You'd need a more advanced implementation to handle such details.
