["Below is a basic example of such a function in C using POSIX threads (pthreads) for thread safety and linked list data structure to manage the cache. Note that this only provides an outline and does not include full implementation due to complexity, but it should give you a good starting point. Also, this code does not handle error handling for data retrieval failures or latency issues which could be very specific and dependent on your actual environment.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct metric {
    int id;
    time_t timestamp;
    float cpuUsage;
    float memoryUsage;
    float networkThroughput;
} Metric;

// Define a node in the linked list
typedef struct node {
    Metric *metric;
    struct node* next;
} Node;

Node *head = NULL; // Head of the linked list
pthread_mutex_t lock;  // Mutex for thread safety

void updateMetric(int id, float cpuUsage, float memoryUsage, float networkThroughput) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);  // Lock to ensure atomicity of operations

    Node* newNode = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (!newNode) {
        exit(-1);  // Memory allocation failed, handle error appropriately.
    }

    Metric *metric = (Metric *)malloc(sizeof(Metric));
    if (!metric) {
        free(newNode);
        exit(-1);  // Memory allocation failed, handle error appropriately.
    }

    metric->id = id;
    time(&metric->timestamp);  // Store current timestamp
    metric->cpuUsage = cpuUsage;
    metric->memoryUsage = memoryUsage;
    metric->networkThroughput = networkThroughput;

    newNode->metric = metric;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;  // New node is now the head of list.

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // Unlock after operations are done
}
```
This code creates a linked list where each node contains a Metric struct and links to the next node in the list. This allows efficient insertion at the beginning, which is what we want when adding new metrics. A mutex lock is used around any modifications to the linked list to ensure thread safety.

In terms of optimizing memory usage, it's important to note that while this code does not optimize for memory use in the most efficient way possible (you could implement a more space-efficient data structure), in many cases, the trade-off between memory usage and update frequency is acceptable when dealing with typical network traffic loads. For example, if you need to store metrics for every second of every day, that would be 86400 metrics per device, which may not fit into available memory even on high-end servers if we consider other processes running on the system. So, it's important to balance these trade-offs based on your specific use case.
