['Here is a simple example of how you can implement this task using the `mmap` library in Python for efficient memory mapping and hashing lines in a file, and JEP (Java Embedded Python) to embed Python into Java:

```java
import java.io.*;
import org.python.util.PythonInterpreter;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.nio.MappedByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.FileChannel;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class Main {
    private static final PythonInterpreter interp = new PythonInterpreter();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        String fileName = "/path/to/your/file";

        FileInputStream stream = new FileInputStream(new File(fileName));
        FileChannel fc = stream.getChannel();

        MappedByteBuffer bb = fc.map(FileChannel.MapMode.READ_ONLY, 0, fc.size());

        if (bb != null) {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            byte[] data = new byte[1];

            for (int i = 0; bb.hasRemaining(); i++) {
                bb.get(data);

                char nextByte = (char) data[0];

                if (nextByte == '\n') {  // Assuming lines are separated by newline character
                    String line = sb.toString();
                    System.out.println("MD5: " + DigestUtils.md5Hex(line));  // Print MD5 hash

                    interp.set("line", line);  
                    interp.exec("print(hashlib.md5(line.encode()).hexdigest())");  // Execute Python code

                    sb = new StringBuilder();
                } else {
                    sb.append((char) data[0]);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In the above Java program, we are first reading the file into a memory mapped buffer to efficiently handle large files and prevent running out of memory. We then loop over each byte in the buffer, appending it to a StringBuilder until we reach a newline character indicating the end of a line in the text file. When this happens, we print the MD5 hash of the current line using Apache Commons's `DigestUtils` and also execute equivalent Python code (which is included as a string) using JEP.

This program does not handle any error conditions explicitly but it can be easily extended to do so. For instance, you might want to add an exception handler around the FileInputStream initialization to catch exceptions related to file access, and similarly for the other I/O operations. You may also want to use thread synchronization constructs (like locks) when accessing shared resources between threads (i.e., Python interpreter).

Please note that using JEP requires a significant amount of setup due to its complex dependencies. The code above is just an example and might not work as expected without the necessary configuration. Moreover, MD5 hashing in Java and Python are different so this program prints two different values for the same input line.

Remember to replace "/path/to/your/file" with the path of your actual file.
']

