['Here is a high-level structure of how we can implement this in Java using Guava's RateLimiter, ConcurrentHashMap for Cache and Logback for logging. For simplicity, I am not handling exceptions or edge cases, but in production code, they should be properly handled. Note that this is just the utility class part without a complete application.

```java
import com.google.common.hash.Hashing;
import com.google.common.util.concurrent.RateLimiter;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class MessageProcessor {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MessageProcessor.class);

    // Cache configuration and initialization
    private ConcurrentMap<String, String> messageCache; 
    private int cacheSize = 10_000;
    private int expireAfterWriteMinutes = 5;

    // Rate limiter configuration
    private double permitsPerSecond = 10.0;
    private RateLimiter rateLimiter = RateLimiter.create(permitsPerSecond);

    public MessageProcessor() {
        this.messageCache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(cacheSize)
                .expireAfterWrite(expireAfterWriteMinutes, TimeUnit.MINUTES)
                .build();
    }

    // Message priority queue
    private PriorityBlockingQueue<Message> messagePriorityQueue = new PriorityBlockingQueue<>(10, Comparator.comparing(Message::getPriority));

    public void processMessages() {
        while (true) {
            if (rateLimiter.tryAcquire()) {
                Message message = messagePriorityQueue.poll(); // This is where you receive messages from a stream

                if(message != null){
                    String md5Hash = computeMD5(message);
                    logger.info("Message: {}, MD5 Hash: {}", message, md5Hash);
                    messageCache.put(message.getContent(), md5Hash); // Cache the result for later use
                }
            } else {
                logger.warn("Rate limit exceeded");
                handleBackpressure(); // Handle back pressure if necessary
            }
        }
    }

    private String computeMD5(Message message) {
        return messageCache.computeIfAbsent(message.getContent(), key -> Hashing.md5()
                .hashString(key, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)
                .toString());
    }

    public void handleBackpressure(){
        // Handle back pressure if necessary
    }
}
``` 

This class includes caching of recently computed MD5 hashes using a ConcurrentHashMap for better concurrency, rate-limiting with Guava's RateLimiter to prevent abuse, and priority based message processing. However, these choices might have implications on performance depending on the specific use case. For instance, a high cache hit ratio would lead to reduced computation time and thus increase throughput but consume more memory. A lower rate limit would decrease the number of messages processed at once and thus improve response times, but may cause temporary overloads or false positives for abusive traffic. 

This design uses a naive approach of discarding old entries in the cache after a fixed amount of time (expireAfterWrite). This could lead to potential data loss if messages are processed quickly enough before their lifetime expires. It's important to choose an appropriate eviction policy based on your specific use case. 

Backpressure handling is left as future work, but it may involve strategies like dropping excess messages, buffering, or applying back-pressure to the producer of incoming messages. This will depend on the nature and requirements of your data pipeline.
']

