Node* head = NULL;
int count = 0, M; // Maximum size of the list

void initializeThirdPartyLibrary() {
    // Assume there is a function in third party library to initialize it 
}

void validateCRC(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* data) {
   if (!validateCRCFromLib(data->CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG.CRCFLAG)) {
       log("Invalid CRC flag detected", ERROR);
   }
}

void addData(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        log("Failed to allocate memory", ERROR);
        return;
    }

    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    if(++count > M){
        Node* temp = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < count - M - 1; ++i) {
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        Node *nodeToRemove = temp->next;
        temp->next = nodeToRemove->next;
        free(nodeToRemove);
    }

    validateCRC(&data);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code creates a linked list where each node holds a `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structure and a pointer to the next node. The addData function is thread-safe using a mutex lock, preventing race conditions when multiple threads access the data at the same time. 

The memory is managed efficiently by only allocating new nodes when necessary and deleting outdated ones once the list reaches maximum size (M). It also includes basic CRC validation and logging capability through a log function, which you should implement yourself based on your requirements for debugging and monitoring purposes. The initializeThirdPartyLibrary() function is just a placeholder for the third party library initialization; replace it with your actual code.
