        return 1;
    }

    RSA* rsa = RSA_new(); // Initialize OpenSSL resources
    EVP_PKEY* pkey = EVP_PKEY_new();

    char commandBuffer[256];
    while (fgets(commandBuffer, sizeof(commandBuffer), input) != NULL) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof(commands)/sizeof(*commands); ++i) {
            if (strncmp(commandBuffer, commands[i].type, strlen(commands[i].type)) == 0) {
                (*commands[i].execute)(commandBuffer, rsa, pkey); // Execute command
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    RSA_free(rsa); // Free OpenSSL resources
    EVP_PKEY_free(pkey);
    fclose(input);

    return 0;
}
```
This example demonstrates the basic structure of what your program might look like. It reads a list of commands from a file, parses them into individual strings, and executes each command based on its type using a dynamically allocated environment.

It uses OpenSSL to handle cryptographic operations, which includes HMAC, SHA-256, AES encryption, and RSA signing. These are all abstracted behind the `executeArithmetic`, `executeStringManipulation`, and `executeFileIO` functions, allowing you to modify these functions without affecting the rest of your program.

The code also shows how to handle OpenSSL resources properly in order to prevent memory leaks or other resource exhaustion issues. You should add proper error handling and possibly extend this example to include additional command types and cryptographic operations as per your requirements.
