['Implementing a full HTTP request header management system with all mentioned properties is a complex task which requires a deep understanding of data structures, thread safety issues, error handling, disk persistence, batch operations, memory management strategies, and other concepts in C language. Here's an outline of how you might start developing such a function:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct HttpHeader {
    char* method;
    char* urlPath;
    char* queryParameters;
    char* contentType;
    char* userAgent;
    struct HttpHeader *next;
} HttpHeader;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
HttpHeader *httpHeadersListHead = NULL;

void addHeader(char* method, char* urlPath, char* queryParameters, char* contentType, char* userAgent) {
    HttpHeader* newNode = malloc(sizeof(HttpHeader));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed");
        return;
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    newNode->method = method;
    newNode->urlPath = urlPath;
    newNode->queryParameters = queryParameters;
    newNode->contentType = contentType;
    newNode->userAgent = userAgent;
    newNode->next = httpHeadersListHead;
    httpHeadersListHead = newNode;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void removeHeader(char* urlPath) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    HttpHeader **tracer = &httpHeadersListHead;

    while(*tracer) {
        if(strcmp((*tracer)->urlPath, urlPath) == 0){
            HttpHeader *nextNode = (*tracer)->next;
            free(*tracer);
            *tracer = nextNode;
            break;
        } else {
            tracer = &((*tracer)->next);
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This is a skeleton of the code, and it's not complete due to its complexity. Each function must be developed in detail according to all requirements. The list operations are protected by mutexes for thread safety, and each added node carries pointers to strings representing header data. A more complex design might involve using a pool allocator for efficient memory usage when handling large amounts of data, or employing other synchronization techniques such as reader-writer locks depending on the specific requirements.
