["Here's a simplified implementation in C. The full implementation would be far more complex due to the intricate details of TCP state management and error handling. Note that this is a very high-level overview and doesn't include many optimizations that you might want for a real production environment, such as memory pooling or lock-free programming.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef enum {
    ESTABLISHED, FIN_WAIT, CLOSE_WAIT, ...
} TCPState;

typedef struct {
    int connectionID;
    char* bufferPointer;
    int flags;
    TCPState state;
} TCPPacket;

typedef struct Node{
    TCPPacket packet;
    struct Node *next;
}Node;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Adding a Packet to list
void addTCPPacket(int connectionID, char* bufferPointer, int flags, TCPState state){
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    Node *newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->packet.connectionID = connectionID;
    newNode->packet.bufferPointer = bufferPointer;
    newNode->packet.flags = flags;
    newNode->packet.state = state;
    newNode->next = NULL;

    Node *temp = head;
    while(temp->next != NULL){
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    temp->next = newNode;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Removing a Packet from list
void removeTCPPacket(int connectionID){
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    Node *temp = head;
    while(temp->next != NULL && temp->packet.connectionID != connectionID){
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    if(temp->next == NULL){
        return;
    }
    Node *nodeToRemove = temp->next;
    temp->next = nodeToRemove->next;
    free(nodeToRemove);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This is just a start, to fully complete this you need much more code including error handling, persistence serialization/deserialization, batch operations and high throughput network traffic optimization. 

Also note that in production level programming it's often recommended to use a library for concurrency or memory management rather than manually implementing the locking mechanism. The C++ standard library provides std::mutex, std::thread, std::shared_ptr, etc. and there are many libraries available like Boost for this kind of task. 

Lastly, remember that writing code in a real-world environment requires proper testing and code review to ensure its correctness, performance, and maintainability. This includes not just unit tests but also load and stress tests, as well as security reviews.
