['Here's a basic implementation using C++11 and pthread mutex for thread safety. 

```c++
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>
#include <ctime>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct LTE_EPC_NODE_T {
    int UE_ID;
    int EPS_BEARER_ID;
    std::string NAS_MESSAGE;
} LTE_EPC_NODE_T;

std::unordered_set<std::string> existingMessages;
std::vector<LTE_EPC_NODE_T> nodeList;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
const int MAX_SIZE = 10000;  // Maximum list size

void addNode(int UE_ID, int EPS_BEARER_ID, std::string NAS_MESSAGE) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (nodeList.size() >= MAX_SIZE) {  // Check list size limit
        printf("Maximum size reached.\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return;
    }

    std::time_t t = std::time(0);  // Get current time
    std::string timestampedMessage = NAS_MESSAGE + " - " + std::ctime(&t);   // Append timestamp to message

    if (existingMessages.count(timestampedMessage)) {  // Check for duplicates
        printf("Duplicate message detected, appended timestamp.\n");
    } else {
        existingMessages.insert(timestampedMessage);  // Add new message to set

        LTE_EPC_NODE_T node = {UE_ID, EPS_BEARER_ID, timestampedMessage};  // Create new node
        nodeList.push_back(node);  // Add new node to list
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code maintains a global unordered set `existingMessages` for quick duplicate detection and an ordered vector `nodeList` for efficient traversal. Thread safety is achieved using mutual exclusion via the `pthread_mutex_t` lock object, ensuring that only one thread can modify these data structures at a time.

However, please note that this solution might have scalability issues as size increases due to the use of an unordered set and vector. If performance becomes an issue with large datasets, consider using different data structures or database solutions for more efficient storage and querying. 

Also, memory allocation/deallocation can be handled by the standard C++ resource management (RAII), which automatically deallocates when an object goes out of scope. However, if you must manually manage memory, ensure to use a smart pointer to automatically handle this for you as per RAII principle. 

This code does not explicitly handle concurrent access from multiple threads as it is dependent on the context in which it runs and how the `addNode` function is used. To ensure thread safety without compromising performance, consider using lock-free data structures or synchronization mechanisms like locks, condition variables, semaphores etc.
