['As this is a comprehensive task and requires substantial development, it's impossible to provide an entire solution here due to space limitations. However, I can give you a high level overview of how such a framework might be structured using Java 8 features like Stream API for lazy evaluation and Future interface for asynchronous processing. This is just the core part:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.function.*;

public class LogBatchProcessor {

    private final int batchSize;
    private final ExecutorService executorService;
    private final List<Consumer<LogEntry>> strategies = new ArrayList<>();
    private final ConcurrentLinkedQueue<Future<?>> tasks = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();

    public LogBatchProcessor(int maxConcurrency, int batchSize) {
        this.batchSize = batchSize;
        executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(maxConcurrency);
    }

    // Register your transformation strategy
    public void registerTransformationStrategy(Consumer<LogEntry> strategy) {
        strategies.add(strategy);
    }

    // Process batch of log entries
    private void processBatch(List<LogEntry> batch) {
        tasks.offer(executorService.submit(() -> {
            for (Consumer<LogEntry> strategy : strategies) {
                batch.stream().forEachOrdered(strategy);  // Lazy evaluation, only processing needed entries
            }
        }));
    }

    public void addLogEntries(List<LogEntry> logEntries) {
        for (int i = 0; i < logEntries.size(); i += batchSize) {
            processBatch(logEntries.subList(i, Math.min(i + batchSize, logEntries.size())));
        }
    }

    // Cleanup after processing or cancellation
    public void shutdownAndAwaitTermination() {
        executorService.shutdown();
        try {
            if (!executorService.awaitTermination(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                executorService.shutdownNow();
                if (!executorService.awaitTermination(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) System.err.println("Executor Service did not terminate");
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException ie) {
            executorService.shutdownNow();
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}
```

This code provides a basic framework for batch processing of log entries using Java 8 features and Future/Executor services for asynchronous processing, error handling, and resource management. It could be extended to include additional features like configurable rules for destination delivery, comprehensive logging, monitoring API, caching mechanism for external dependencies, automated performance testing, etc., depending on the requirements.

Please remember that writing such a complex framework requires deep understanding of Java concurrency primitives, best practices for managing resources and error handling, among other things. The above code does not cover all possible scenarios or edge cases, but it should be a good starting point for you to expand upon.
