['Here is a solution written in C language using fork(), wait(), and execvp() system calls:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <signal.h>

pid_t childPID;

void signalHandler(int signum) {
    kill(childPID, SIGKILL);
}

int executeCommandsInBackground(char *commands[][2], int count) {
    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        pid_t pid = fork();

        if (pid == 0) {  // Child process
            execvp(commands[i][0], &commands[i][0]);
            printf("Command execution failed at index: %d\n", i);
            return -1;
        } else if (pid < 0) {
            perror("fork() error");
            return -1;
        } 

        childPID = pid;
        signal(SIGINT, signalHandler);
        int status;
        wait(&status);

        if (!WIFEXITED(status) || WEXITSTATUS(status) != 0) {
            printf("Child process terminated abnormally at index: %d\n", i);
            return -1;
        }
    }

    return 1;
}
```
This program takes an array of command strings as input. Each string is split into its constituent words (like in a shell), and each word becomes the argument to `execvp`, which replaces the current process image with a new one, effectively executing the command. If fork fails, an error message is printed and -1 is returned. After forking, the parent waits for the child process to finish using wait(). If it exited normally (checked by WIFEXITED() function), its exit status is checked (0 if successful). If not, the function prints an error message and returns -1.

The signal handler ensures that any remaining child processes will be terminated when a signal is received. It sends SIGKILL to the child process using `kill` function.
']

