['Here is a C code snippet that meets the specified requirements. Note that this is a simplified version of what could be a complex system and may not run out of the box without additional dependencies or libraries. Please adjust according to your project's needs. This example uses pthread for thread safety, but actual implementation might vary based on operating system:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    char clientIP[16];  // IP address can be stored as a string in the form "xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx"
    int clientPort;
    char serverIP[16];  // Same as above
    int serverPort;
    int status;         // Connection status: 0 - not established, 1 - successfully established
} TCP_REQUEST_T;

#define MAX_POOL_SIZE 1024     // Maximum number of connections in the pool
#define DEBUG_LOG      0      // Debug log flag. Set to 1 to enable logs, 0 to disable.
static int count = 0;         // Count of active connections
static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;    // Mutex for thread safety
static TCP_REQUEST_T pool[MAX_POOL_SIZE];                  // Connection request pool

int addRequest(const char *clientIP, int clientPort, const char *serverIP, int serverPort) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);   // Lock mutex for thread safety

    if (count >= MAX_POOL_SIZE) {  // Check if pool is full
        if (DEBUG_LOG) printf("Error: Pool size exceeded.\n");
        return -1;  // Return error code if max capacity is reached
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {   // Verify that client IP address is unique
        if (!strcmp(pool[i].clientIP, clientIP)) {
            if (DEBUG_LOG) printf("Error: Duplicate client IP found.\n");
            return -1;  // Return error code for duplicate IPs
        }
    }

    TCP_REQUEST_T newRequest = {.status = 0};   // Create a new request structure with status set to "not established"
    strcpy(newRequest.clientIP, clientIP);    // Store the client and server addresses
    newRequest.clientPort = clientPort;
    strcpy(newRequest.serverIP, serverIP);
    newRequest.serverPort = serverPort;

    pool[count++] = newRequest;  // Add new request to pool

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);   // Unlock mutex after operation is finished
    return count - 1;             // Return index of added connection in the pool
}
```
This code provides a basic implementation for managing a TCP connections pool. It uses locks to ensure thread safety, checks if the maximum capacity has been reached before adding new requests and verifies that client IP addresses are unique. In addition, it includes an option to enable or disable debug logs. 

The function `addRequest` accepts parameters describing a new TCP connection request (client IP address, port number, server IP address, and port number) and returns the index of the added connection in the pool if successful, or -1 in case of errors. 

It's important to note that actual implementation would be more complex and require consideration for load balancing strategies, network communication framework integration, error handling, logging mechanism, performance optimization and more as specified above.
']

