['Here is a simple implementation of what you asked for, please adjust it according to your needs. This solution uses ConcurrentHashMap for thread safety in a multi-threaded environment, where each log entry is represented by a LogEntry object which has timestamp, category and level fields. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentLinkedQueue;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class RollingAverageLogger {
    private final ConcurrentLinkedQueue<LogEntry> logEntries = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();
    private final Set<String> categoriesToInclude = Collections.synchronizedSet(new HashSet<>());
    private long rollingAverage = 0;
    private int retentionPeriodMillis;
    private String thresholdLevel;
    private int minimumNumberOfEntries;

    public RollingAverageLogger(int retentionPeriod, String thresholdLevel, int minimumNumberOfEntries) {
        this.retentionPeriodMillis = retentionPeriod * 1000; // Convert to milliseconds
        this.thresholdLevel = thresholdLevel;
        this.minimumNumberOfEntries = minimumNumberOfEntries;
    }

    public void addLogEntry(long timestamp, String category, String level) {
        LogEntry logEntry = new LogEntry(timestamp, category, level);

        synchronized (logEntries) {
            if (isInWindow(logEntry)) {
                logEntries.add(logEntry);

                while (!isValid()) {
                    logEntries.poll();
                }

                if (shouldBeIncluded(logEntry)) {
                    rollingAverage = calculateRollingAverage();
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public long getCurrentRollingAverage() {
        return rollingAverage;
    }

    public void addCategoryToInclude(String category) {
        categoriesToInclude.add(category);
    }

    private boolean isValid() {
        return logEntries.size() >= minimumNumberOfEntries && calculateRollingAverage() != 0;
    }

    private long calculateRollingAverage() {
        if (logEntries.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        List<Long> timestamps = logEntries.stream()
                .filter(this::shouldBeIncluded)
                .map(LogEntry::getTimestamp).collect(Collectors.toList());

        long sum = 0;
        for (long timestamp : timestamps) {
            sum += timestamp;
        }

        return sum / timestamps.size();
    }

    private boolean isInWindow(LogEntry logEntry) {
        return logEntry.getTimestamp() >= System.currentTimeMillis() - retentionPeriodMillis;
    }

    private boolean shouldBeIncluded(LogEntry logEntry) {
        return logEntry.getLevel().compareToIgnoreCase(thresholdLevel) >= 0  // Log level above threshold
                && (categoriesToInclude.isEmpty() || categoriesToInclude.contains(logEntry.getCategory()));
    }

    private static class LogEntry {
        private final long timestamp;
        private final String category;
        private final String level;

        public LogEntry(long timestamp, String category, String level) {
            this.timestamp = timestamp;
            this.category = category;
            this.level = level;
        }

        public long getTimestamp() {
            return timestamp;
        }

        public String getCategory() {
            return category;
        }

        public String getLevel() {
            return level;
        }
    }
}
```
This code assumes that all log levels are ordered (e.g., "DEBUG" < "INFO" < "ERROR"), and it doesn't handle invalid timestamps or empty input collections. It also does not account for the possibility of entries being added to the sliding window faster than they expire, which could lead to an overload in memory usage. To fix this, you might want to limit the number of log entries that can be added within a given time period.
']

